// `println(stderr, ...)` and `print(stderr, ...)` write to standard error, as C++23's `std::println(stderr, ...)` does, each
// call's text in one piece, after what standard output has waiting. `stdout` says standard output, as nothing does.
int main() {
    int line = 3;
    println("{} lines", 2);
    println(stderr, "line {}: bad amount {:>6}|", line, "x1");
    print(stderr, "no new line, ");
    print(stderr, "then {}", "one");
    println(stderr);
    println(stdout, "to {} {:.1f}", "stdout", 2.25);
    vector<Task<void>> tasks;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) tasks.push_back(spawn [=] { println(stderr, "thread {} of {}", i, 3); }());
    for (auto& t : tasks) t.wait();
    return 0;
}
// expect: 2 lines
// expect: to stdout 2.2
// stderr: line 3: bad amount     x1|
// stderr: no new line, then one
// stderr: thread 0 of 3
// stderr: thread 2 of 3
